Title:  ‘All your disembodied little heads floating on a screen’: Liveness During the Pandemic in Mel Bradley’s Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins (2020) 
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/19044/
Content: ## Abstract

The outbreak of the Covid-19 pandemic in the UK and Ireland in 2020 and the subsequent lockdowns caused a radical rupture in the world of live performances. Both theatre and poetry performance events moved online in various formats. One of th

**********Extracted claims**********

The Covid-19 pandemic and subsequent lockdowns in the UK and Ireland in 2020 caused a significant disruption to live performances.
Theatre and poetry performance events shifted to online formats during the pandemic.
A central challenge for pandemic performance was the absence of traditional liveness, as audiences and performers could not occupy the same physical space.
Poet-performers developed responses to the lack of traditional liveness in their performances during the pandemic.
The article examines a digital performance by Northern Irish spoken word artist Mel Bradley, titled *Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins* (2020).
*Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins* is situated at the intersection of poetry performance and theatre.
The show adapted to digital performance circumstances by utilizing both livestreamed and prerecorded material.
This adaptation destabilized the traditional boundaries between live and mediatized performance.
The show expanded the possibilities for creating a sense of liveness in the digital sphere.
The unstable co-presence between performers and spectators in the show enhances its themes of punishment, disconnection, and voyeurism.
The themes of the show resonate with the pandemic context in which it was performed.
Theatre and poetry performance have historically been defined as live art forms.
The co-presence of audience and performers in the same physical space at the same time is considered fundamental to traditional theatre and poetry performance.
Liveness has conventionally been contrasted with recorded or filmed material.
This binary positions the relationship between live and mediatized performance as one of opposition, with live performance often seen as superior.
Poet-performers responded quickly and creatively to the restrictions of the pandemic.
Poet-performers expanded the definition of liveness in poetry performance through their practices during the pandemic.
Poet-performers used the structures of livestreaming to explore the shared condition of isolation and social distance enforced upon audiences and performers during the pandemic.
Closed venues, lockdown restrictions, social distancing, and health regulations led to the near-total cessation of in-person performances in March 2020 in many countries.
The arts sector in the UK and Ireland suffered greatly, with an estimated loss of 15,000 performances within 12 weeks from mid-March 2020.
Artists and cultural workers were significantly impacted financially during the pandemic.
Schemes were introduced to support individuals and organizations in the arts across the UK and the Republic of Ireland.
Poetry performance events were disrupted due to venue closures.
The crisis of the pandemic was approached as an incentive for innovation and a motor for creativity by artists.
Artists devised innovative ways to exploit available options, primarily online performances, during the pandemic.
A more expansive understanding of liveness is beneficial for analyzing livestreamed poetry performances.
Philip Auslander's concept of liveness as an historically contingent category is particularly relevant for performance during the pandemic.
Performances during the pandemic employed new strategies to signal different facets of liveness outside of in-person events.
These strategies utilized the affordances of various digital and social media platforms.
Livestreamed performances incorporating prerecorded material challenged the opposition between liveness and mediatization.
Claudia Georgi's work on the use of media in theatre and the incorporation of film and video technologies in in-person British theatre events is expanded upon.
Georgi separates liveness into five interconnected criteria: co-presence, ephemerality, unpredictability/risk of imperfection, possibility of interaction, and a specific quality of the representation of reality.
Georgi argues that co-presence, ephemerality, and unpredictability/risk of imperfection define liveness.
The criteria Georgi identifies as related to liveness can inform critical analyses of livestreamed performances, especially those combining livestreams with prerecorded components.
The article uses a case study of Mel Bradley’s 2020 poetry show *Ms Noir’s Seven Deadly Sins* (*7DS*).
Mel Bradley is a multidisciplinary artist based in Derry, Northern Ireland.
Bradley has been active in the poetry performance scene in Northern Ireland and the Republic of Ireland since 2009.
Bradley has performed at various festivals and events, including Body & Soul, Lingo, Stendhal, and the All-Ireland Poetry Slam.
Bradley has written and performed longer poetry performance shows *7DS* (2016–2020) and *For the Love of Mary* (2022, 2023).
Bradley also writes for theatre.
*7DS* is an innovative poetry performance that incorporates theatrical practices such as sets, props, costumes, burlesque aesthetics, and digital technologies.
*7DS* foregrounds voiced poetry.
The article contributes to broadening the understanding of poetry performance and research, particularly in relation to spoken word theatre.
Poetry shows, especially Northern Irish poetry performances, are underrepresented and under-researched despite a vibrant scene.
*7DS* is a suitable text for analysis because it allows for an investigation of co-temporality and co-presence in livestreams and simulated presences within digital performance.
*7DS* incorporates prerecorded material and pseudo fourth wall breaking strategies.
The show’s themes of punishment, disconnection, and voyeurism gained new dimensions and immediate relevance during the Covid-19 pandemic restrictions in October 2020.
*7DS* was livestreamed on October 29th, 2020, as part of the online Derry Halloween festival.
The Derry Halloween festival is Europe’s largest Halloween festival.
The show was re-conceived multiple times in 2020 due to changing pandemic restrictions.
The final format of the show was a livestream with prerecorded elements.
The show was marketed as a fusion of spoken word, poetry, storytelling, burlesque, and theatre.
The performance lasts 50 minutes.
Ms Noir, Bradley’s burlesque persona and the ‘Keeper of the Keys’ in ‘Hotel Hell,’ tells seven tales about people representing or falling prey to the biblical seven deadly sins.
Bradley’s parts in the show were livestreamed.
Scenes featuring the Caretaker figure, played by Denzil Browne, were prerecorded.
An unpublished recording of the performance was provided by Bradley for analysis.
The analysis combines context-sensitive close viewing techniques for poetry performance with terminology from film analysis.
The analysis of the show is supplemented by interviews with Bradley conducted in 2022 and 2023.
Interviews with Bradley covered her craft, her view of the spoken word scene, and the *7DS* performance.
The article historicizes and theorizes liveness before and during the pandemic in relation to both theatre and poetry performance.
The section ‘Virtually Together: Spatio-Temporal Co-Presence’ argues that co-presence took different forms during the pandemic.
The idea of spatial tele-presence through conferencing and livestreaming technology is proposed.
The section ‘Fleeting Faults: Ephemerality, Uniqueness, Imperfections’ questions differences between livestreamed and prerecorded material.
The blurred boundaries between livestreamed and prerecorded categories are demonstrated.
The section ‘Interaction and Reality: Possibilities, Limitations’ argues that pandemic performance offered alternative forms of audience engagement.
The analysis in the article connects facets of liveness with the substance of the poetry show.
The analysis demonstrates how the ‘hellish’ setting of the show reflects the real-world circumstance of lockdown.
The observations in this study aim to expand analyses of other livestreamed spoken word performances that emerged during the pandemic.
The study intends to contribute to the critical understanding of intermedial strategies, spatial tele-presence, and audience engagement with livestreamed performance.

