Title: Re-visioning the Archive: Cecilia Vicuña’s Permanent Impermanence 
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/18449/
Content: ## Abstract

This essay examines ‘permanent impermanence’ as a key aesthetic to the work of Chilean poet and artist Cecilia Vicuña. It traces Vicuña’s persistent making, unmaking, and remaking of what she has termed her _arte precario_ from the 1960s to t

**********Extracted claims**********

The essay examines ‘permanent impermanence’ as a key aesthetic in the work of Chilean poet and artist Cecilia Vicuña.
The essay traces Cecilia Vicuña’s persistent making, unmaking, and remaking of her _arte precario_ from the 1960s to the present.
The essay considers Cecilia Vicuña’s practice of re-versioning as a form of radical archivism.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is informed by socialist and Indigenous practices of community and care.
Metaphor is an animating concept in the essay, brought back to its root meaning of _carrying over_.
Collectivity is an animating concept in the essay, understood through Cristina Rivera Garza’s work as the bringing together of objects, bodies, and communities in opposition to capitalist individualism.
The essay explores how Cecilia Vicuña builds an alternative narrativization of history.
Cecilia Vicuña builds an alternative narrativization of history by accreting a disappearing, reappearing, collective archive of work.
In Cecilia Vicuña’s work, ephemerality and vulnerability insist as powerful forces.
The essay argues for the aesthetics of permanent impermanence as a theory of how art can respond to and re-vision a world increasingly patterned by the throwaway culture of late capitalism and ongoing histories of colonial, imperial violence.
Cecilia Vicuña first arrived at the concept of _arte precario_ in January 1966 on the beach of Concón.
Concón is where the Aconcagua River meets the Pacific Ocean.
Cecilia Vicuña describes the moment of conceptualizing _arte precario_ in terms of cooperative relation.
Cecilia Vicuña stated that she was doing her sand drawing not for herself but for the sea and for the elements.
Cecilia Vicuña collected beach debris, assembling twigs, feathers, discarded plastic, cloth, and glass into small, fragile sculptures.
Cecilia Vicuña tied her sculptures with bark and string and planted them in the sand along the shoreline.
Reciprocity was the impetus for Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_.
Impermanence was the point of Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_.
Cecilia Vicuña’s sand drawings would be erased by water and wind.
Cecilia Vicuña’s little sculptures, termed _basuritas_, would be carried away by the high tide.
The words ‘prayer’ and ‘precarious’ share an etymological root.
The term ‘precarious’ means what is obtained by prayer, uncertain, exposed to hazards, and insecure.
Cecilia Vicuña’s precarious and ephemeral constructions were offerings.
Cecilia Vicuña’s constructions were transformative acts.
Cecilia Vicuña’s constructions were meaningful assemblages of materials deemed valueless in the language and logics of late capitalism.
Cecilia Vicuña believes that humans are all _basuritas_ or throwaways of this world.
The event at Concón in January 1966 was an act of return, including physical turning, creative recycling, reciprocal perception, and an offering of gratitude.
Cecilia Vicuña’s description of the experience at Concón emphasizes accumulation.
Cecilia Vicuña returned to the same beach the next day and made more sculptures and markings.
_Arte precario_ became the method and concept of Cecilia Vicuña’s artistic and poetic praxis from that point forward.
Cecilia Vicuña's understanding of the Concón moment was an arrival at a familiar, previously inhabited consciousness.
Cecilia Vicuña felt a connection on the beach with an ancestral drive stemming from the site’s pre-Columbian past.
Concón was named by the first people of Chile after its meeting place of water and water.
According to Cecilia Vicuña, Concón is a place with no historical memory, yet with a deep memory embedded in the land.
Cecilia Vicuña describes Concón as an inclusive site of spatial and geological memory.
Concón activated within Cecilia Vicuña a recognition that all elements of the world are brought into being reciprocally.
Cecilia Vicuña’s ethics and aesthetics have been shaped by an emphasis on interactive response rather than singular originality from the 1960s to the present.
Cecilia Vicuña acknowledges the influence of the credo to _make it new_ in her early education.
Cecilia Vicuña asserts that whatever artists do is always a continuation.
Cecilia Vicuña states that there is a permanent impermanence to the relationship between an artist and their work.
Cecilia Vicuña’s approach resists the hierarchical schema entrenched in capitalist value systems.
Cecilia Vicuña refuses the guiding logic of individualism.
Cecilia Vicuña states that everything people do responds to something they are building on.
The most important aspect of Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ is the set of relations that the artworks and poems (re)animate.
The relations in Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ exist internally between elements of construction and externally between the artwork, its environment, and its witnesses/readers.
Cecilia Vicuña states that an object is not an object, but a witness to a relationship.
Cecilia Vicuña extended her practice of recycling and re-versioning to her writing.
Cecilia Vicuña reproduces and alters poetic fragments, manifesto extracts, performance notes, and photographs of her site-specific and installation pieces across various publications.
Cecilia Vicuña’s entire oeuvre has been and continues to be a project of radical archivism.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is an alternative method of documentation that counters dominant historiographies.
Dominant historiographies are constructed primarily to dismantle the relations Cecilia Vicuña works to reactivate and to silence narratives she wishes to amplify.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is a relational project.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is shaped by principles of communality and participation.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is in contradistinction to conventional standards of archiving that privilege custodial and preservationist paradigms.
Cecilia Vicuña’s radical archivism is more aligned with collective, activist-driven models of archivism that began in the late 1960s.
Activist-driven models of archivism are predicated on social responsibility and equality.
Activist-driven models of archivism position the archivist as a community facilitator.
Understanding Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics of permanent impermanence involves inquiring how an archival practice might value underrepresented and undervalued people, ecologies, cultures, and communities.
Understanding Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics of permanent impermanence involves inquiring how an archive’s organizing structure might reflect the precarity of its subjects.
Cecilia Vicuña was born in 1948.
Cecilia Vicuña came of age during an era when conceptual artistic movements in the States and Latin America were taking root.
During Cecilia Vicuña’s formative era, cultural production intersected with political and social life, positioning art as an open system and matrix of social exchange.
Over the last 20 years, scholars have paid increasing attention to the work of Latin American artists and poets responding to violent political regimes.
Much scholarship has focused on cultural production during Brazil's 21-year dictatorship (1964–1985).
Artists and poets like Helio Oiticica, Lygia Clark, Antonio Manuel, Artur Barrio, Cildo Meireles, and Ferreira Gullar created art objects, poems, and performances during the Brazilian dictatorship that blurred text and image, emphasized viewer participation, and sought new formulations of democracy.
Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ can be placed within the lineage of these Latin American artistic movements.
Chilean art movements of the 1960s did not embrace conceptualism as readily as some other regions.
Scholar Juliet Lynd states that Cecilia Vicuña was on the vanguard of the Chilean cultural scene in the 1960s and 70s.
Cecilia Vicuña’s visual, performative, and poetic work of that era anticipated important artistic and academic movements in conceptual art and performance of the 70s and 80s, including discourses of second-wave feminism.
Lucy Lippard classified Cecilia Vicuña’s small sculptures made in Concón in 1966 as a precursor by six months to Robert Smithson’s Earthworks in the US.
Cecilia Vicuña’s Concón sculptures also predate the silhouettes of Ana Mendieta.
Cecilia Vicuña’s Concón sculptures predate the earth-drawings made by Atsuko Tanaka in Japan in 1968.
Cecilia Vicuña left Chile in 1972 to study art in London.
Cecilia Vicuña was exiled after the 1973 coup in Chile.
Cecilia Vicuña lived in London, then Bogotá, and has lived in New York since 1980.
Collectivity is a key concept to Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_.
Collectivity is the method of construction Cecilia Vicuña uses to assemble the component parts of her _basuritas_, artist’s books, and _quipu_ installations.
Collectivity in Cecilia Vicuña’s work extends to include communities of people and their more-than-human relations.
Mexican writer Cristina Rivera Garza argues that authorship is always plural.
Cristina Rivera Garza argues that writing and cultural production are acts of ‘communality’ in which both the living and the dead partake.
For Cristina Rivera Garza, disappropriation is an ethical and aesthetic practice that exposes the plurality preceding individuality in the creative process.
Disappropriation reveals the material layering often concealed by appropriative texts.
Disappropriation’s way of ‘appearing’ is usually attained by means of many different re-writing strategies, including excavation, recycling, and juxtaposition.
Cristina Rivera Garza draws on the Indigenous Oaxacan practice of _tequio_.
_Tequio_ is a form of collective labor concerned with the material creation and recreation of the world.
_Tequio_ benefits the community at large.
Cristina Rivera Garza's ‘poetics of communality’ derives from the Mixe philosophy of ‘relationship with the earth based not on property but on mutual belonging’.
Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics derive from Indigenous understandings of relationality, specifically Quechua, in which all forms of life and nature are interwoven.
Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_ is informed by Andean concepts such as _minga_.
_Minga_ is a pre-Incan practice that brings community members together to work for the common good.
Metaphor is a key concept to Cecilia Vicuña’s _arte precario_.
Cecilia Vicuña has often called the _precarios_ ‘visual poems’ and ‘metaphors in space’.
The reconfigured forms of _precarios_ afford them capacity to hold a plurality of meanings.
Metaphor has roots in the Latin and Greek _metaphora_, meaning ‘carrying over’.
Metaphor involves the ‘transfer’ of an object or word from one location or condition to another.
An ‘alteration’ occurs during the process of metaphor.
Translation also means to ‘carry over’ or to ‘remove from one place to another’.
Cecilia Vicuña knows the disquieting linguistic disorientation of migration.
Much of Cecilia Vicuña’s poetry in Spanish has been translated by Rosa Alcalá and Daniel Borzutzky.
Cecilia Vicuña writes in Spanish and English.
Cecilia Vicuña maintains that ‘language’ is ‘migrant’.
Many of Cecilia Vicuña’s poems are translingual.
Cecilia Vicuña’s translingual poems move across and between Spanish and English to explore commonalities, reflect on linguistic displacement, and unpick multiple meanings embedded in words.
The concepts of collectivity and metaphor provide related ways to examine Cecilia Vicuña’s aesthetics of permanent impermanence.

