Title: Homosexual Panic and Necropolitics in The Picture of Dorian Gray and ‘A Painful Case’
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16916/
Content: ## Abstract

This article provides a comparison between James Joyce and Oscar Wilde’s characters in matters of homosexual panic and gender inequality, bringing the analysis to the grounds of necropolitics. Coined by Achille  Mbembe, the term ‘necropolitic

**********Extracted claims**********

This article compares James Joyce’s and Oscar Wilde’s characters regarding homosexual panic and gender inequality.
The analysis in this article applies the concept of necropolitics.
Achille Mbembe coined the term ‘necropolitics’.
‘Necropolitics’ refers to how certain lives are considered expendable within neoliberal systems.
Legal and social oppression of queer people and gender minorities in the late-19th and early-20th centuries made queer people and women vulnerable to social annihilation.
The criminalization of homosexuality was linked to homosexual panic.
Homosexual panic is a manipulative and necropolitical mechanism.
Homosexual panic drove queer people to construct and maintain heteronormative appearances.
Oscar Wilde’s trials triggered homosexual panic.
Homosexual panic resulted in tightened social control and isolative measures.
These measures largely targeted homosexual and bisexual men.
Basil Hallward’s murder and the suicides of Dorian Gray’s lovers in Wilde’s _The Picture of Dorian Gray_ are manifestations of the effects of homosexual panic.
James Duffy’s isolation in Joyce’s ‘A Painful Case’ is a manifestation of the effects of homosexual panic.
Homosexual panic forces Joyce’s and Wilde’s queer characters into a ‘living dead’ status unless they comply with an oppressive system.
Social control and puritanism also affected women.
These factors limited the roles women could occupy in society, the workplace, and the home.
This article analyzes the suicides of Emily Sinico and Sibyl Vane.
This analysis demonstrates how gendered necropolitics impacted their deaths beyond mere tragic love stories.
Perceptions of Oscar Wilde’s works have changed significantly over time.
_The Picture of Dorian Gray_ (1890) was initially considered scandalous.
_The Picture of Dorian Gray_ was used as evidence of gross indecency against Oscar Wilde.
_The Picture of Dorian Gray_ is now regularly introduced to readers as a canonically gay novel.
Attitudes towards homosexuality began to shift by the time James Joyce’s _Dubliners_ (1916) was published.
This shift was a direct result of Oscar Wilde’s sensationalized persecution.
This article analyzes Joyce’s and Wilde’s characters through the lens of necropolitics.
The article examines how homosexual panic and gender inequality contributed to tragic, preventable deaths of women and gay men.
Both Joyce and Wilde criticize the strict moral codes that governed the public and private lives of ‘sexual deviants’ oppressed within a heterosexist system.
Factors that can be deduced from the era's proscription of same-sex relationships, colonial situation, and gender imbalances promoted by strict moral codes influenced their criticism.
‘Necropolitics’ refers to how neoliberal systems exhaust lives that do not contribute to them.
Necropolitics is inspired by the Foucauldian concept of biopolitics.
Achille Mbembe reorients biopolitics to center the power to expose the body to death and to a kind of death-in-life.
In biopolitics, genocides are justified through the point of view of preserving nations.
In biopolitics, racism is considered the dividing component of the human species.
Necropolitics focuses on how individuals are subjected to ‘slow deaths’ in the contemporary era.
The climate crisis, mass deportations, and the development of spaces of confinement and surveillance are circumstances in what Mbembe calls ‘death-worlds’.
In ‘death-worlds,’ vast populations are subjected to living conditions that confer upon them the status of the living dead.
Since necropolitics revolves around colonial violence, it is a suitable lens for the analysis of Joyce’s texts.
Joyce’s texts were written when Ireland was under British rule.
Late modern colonial occupation differs in many ways from early modern occupation.
The semi-colonial situation in Ireland parallels late modern colonial contexts.
Ireland displayed both compliance and resistance towards the colonial force from the mid-19th century.
These sympathies did not prevent the British government from employing racial, linguistic, and cultural cleansing methods.
These methods ultimately rendered the colonized ‘disposable’.
Edward Molloy states that the mass production of death, made possible by both the demands of British capital and the racialization of the Irish masses, shows how mechanisms of colonial domination institute death as the defining characteristic of the colonial experience.
Colonial Ireland fits within a necropolitical context.
The Great Famine is a representative example of a death-world in Ireland.
Necropolitics has been applied to multiple areas to justify that different forms of oppression and violence reduce individuals’ quality of life.
Necropolitics can be applied to women.
Women have historically been victims of sexism and gendered violence.
Women are more susceptible to murder than their male counterparts.
Mbembe does not extensively discuss the role of gender and sexuality in necropolitics.
Gendered violence and death make up a fundamental element of contemporary death-dealing.
Movements such as antifemicide movements demonstrate subversions and resistances to operations of death-dealing.
In the context of turn-of-the-century Ireland, gendered necropolitics can be linked to the mechanisms of control promoted by strict moral codes.
These codes diminished women’s opportunities to live beyond their assigned traditional gender roles.
The prevalence of institutions such as the Magdalene laundries in Ireland is a tragic and sensitive example.
These laundries incarcerated women under pretexts of social purity and cleansing.
This article will analyze Emily Sinico’s and Sibyl Vane’s suicides under the perspective of gendered necropolitics.
Gendered necropolitics offers an expanded view of the politics of death that also includes the perspective of Queer Studies.
Thinking through necropolitics on the terrain of queer critique brings everyday death worlds into view.
These death worlds range from expected sites of death making to the ordinary and completely normalized violence of the market.
Most analyses of queer necropolitics focus on current affairs.
Deaths attributable to sexual orientation and gender identity are not unique to the contemporary era.
Homosexual panic in Wilde’s and Joyce’s time can be understood as a reaction to institutionalized homophobia and violence.
This institutionalized homophobia and violence deeply affected homosexual people’s behavior and daily lives.
Homosexual panic contains necropolitical implications.
During the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, distinct categories like homosexual and heterosexual were not recognized in the same way as today.
Homophobia was institutionalized, becoming a legal measure for social control after 1885.
This institutionalization is reflected in legislation such as Section 11 of the Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885 in the United Kingdom.
Homosexual relationships qualified as acts of ‘gross indecency’.
Those convicted of ‘gross indecency’ faced imprisonment for any term not exceeding two years, with or without hard labour.
This legislation was a response to social rejection following ‘sex scandals’.
The Dublin Castle scandal of 1884 involved people being judged in terms of indecency and sodomy.
The Cleveland Street Affair in London 1889 introduced a heated atmosphere of hysteria.
These scandals spread a wave of sexual blackmail.
Sexual blackmail caused homosexual and bisexual people to become aware of their identity.
They were then silenced with fear of discovery.
Aversion towards dissident sexualities and practices raised anxiogenic responses in the population who practiced prohibited sexual acts.
Social rejection was accompanied by legal consequences.
Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick coined the term ‘homosexual panic’ in _Epistemology of the Closet_ (1990).
Sedgwick describes these anxieties as a structural residue of terrorist potential and blackmailability of Western maleness through the leverage of homophobia.
Joseph Valente (1994), Patricia Smith (1995), and Roberta Jackson (1999) have employed other terms such as ‘gay panic’ and ‘lesbian panic’.
These terms describe environments in fiction where characters are unable to acknowledge their sexual desires due to their fear of losing social status and reputation.
Under the effects of homosexual panic, homosexual and bisexual people were forced to conceal their identities and maintain heterosexual appearances.
James Joyce suffered the consequences of this ambient homosexual panic.
James Joyce was careful with his words when writing about Oscar Wilde.
Margot Norris states that the young Joyce brothers were disturbed by the sex scandals that had shaken the Irish political and literary landscape during their youth.
Joyce complained about the veiled homosexual connotations of _The Picture of Dorian Gray_.
Joyce avoided conversation about Wilde’s sexuality through euphemisms.
Joyce did not comment on Wilde’s sexuality in his 1909 review of the Trieste production of _Salomé_.
Joyce euphemistically named Wilde’s sexuality a ‘sad mania’ and ‘strange problem’.
Joyce attributed Wilde’s sexuality to the logical and inevitable product of the Anglo-Saxon college and university system, a system of seclusion and secrecy.
Joyce himself experienced this educational system.
Joyce criticized Wilde in 1909 for not having the ‘courage’ to ‘develop the allusions’ to homosexuality.
Modern readers will understand that Wilde was forced to censor the novel’s homoeroticism.
This censorship was an attempt to avoid the legal and social persecution that would soon ruin Wilde.
Male homosexual panic became the normal condition of male heterosexual entitlement.
The maintenance of the heterosexual appearance becomes a priority.
This priority justifies acts of violence when heterosexual and masculine images are threatened.
Sedgwick explains that the contradictions between encouraging male bonds while avoiding homosocial desires result in acute manipulability through the fear of one’s own ‘homosexuality’.
This also results in a reservoir of potential for violence caused by the self-ignorance that this regime constitutively enforces.
Since homosexual individuals were forbidden from living as their true identities and vulnerable to social rejection and legal prosecution, homosexual panic is a consequence of necropolitics.
The lives of these individuals existed in a state akin to the ‘living dead’.
Institutional homophobia, reflected in measures such as Section 11 of the Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885, depicted homosexual people as criminals.
This homophobia encouraged practices such as sexual blackmail.
This demonstrates a necropolitical power that sustained social control and supervision.
Victims of these legal measures were immersed in a state of constant insecurity, anxiety and fear.
Their overall wellbeing was threatened.
Their living conditions conferred upon them the status of the _living dead_.
Joyce acknowledged Wilde’s ‘living dead’ status in his review of _Salomé_.
Joyce compared Wilde, prosecuted under the 1885 Act for ‘gross indecency’, to an undead son of Ossian who met his ‘civil death’ while discussing Plato.
Margot Gayle Backus states that Wilde’s social exile and the punitive removal of his children, his ‘civil death’, was seen by Joyce as stripping from Wilde that which rendered him recognizably human.
In _A Picture of Dorian Gray_, the actions of queer necropolitics are noticeable in Basil Hallward’s attitude and consequent murder.
The actions of queer necropolitics are also noticeable in the suicide cases of Dorian Gray’s lovers.

