Title: Plesaunt was his absolucioun? Friars and Light Penances in English History and Literature
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16931/
Content: ## Abstract

Friars in late-medieval English literature are often figures of hypocrisy. One theme, most familiar from Chaucer, is the friar who hears confessions of the laity and assigns light penances for serious sins with the expectation that the penite

**********Extracted claims**********

Friars in late-medieval English literature are often depicted as hypocritical figures.
A common theme in literature, particularly in Chaucer, describes a friar who hears confessions of the laity and assigns light penances for serious sins while expecting generous donations in return.
This literary image of friars has been interpreted as representing historical reality.
The evidence for friars assigning lighter penances than secular clergy, especially to attract donations, is remarkably thin.
Even where there are reasons to suspect friars of behaving in this way, strong evidence indicates that secular (parish) clergy also engaged in similar practices.
The fully-developed version of the accusation regarding friars giving light penances emerged in the 1350s.
Richard FitzRalph, Archbishop of Armagh, was responsible for the emergence of the fully-developed accusation against friars.
Richard FitzRalph was an opponent of the friars.
Richard FitzRalph was a member of a small group of severe moral critics among the senior clergy.
FitzRalph combined multiple strands of existing anti-fraternal critiques and an agenda shaped by his Irish context.
FitzRalph launched a propaganda campaign.
FitzRalph's propaganda campaign influenced English bishops and writers.
After FitzRalph’s campaign, accusations against friars reflect confirmation bias on the part of observers.
Accusations against friars made after FitzRalph’s campaign cannot be trusted by historians for accuracy.
Geoffrey Chaucer described his pilgrim Friar Huberd as having more power of confession than a curate, being a licentiate of his order, hearing confession sweetly, giving pleasant absolution, and being an easy man to give penance when he expected a good pittance.
Gower’s _Vox Clamantis_ and Langland’s _Piers Plowman_ made charges very similar to Chaucer's description of Friar Huberd.
Two anonymous Wycliffite poems of the period claimed that friars would shrive a man for a sixpence or for less than the cost of a pair of shoes, even if the man murdered his family.
Historians have echoed criticisms of friars, with varying degrees of caution.
In 1908, George Gordon Coulton quoted _Piers Plowman_ on friars giving false absolutions and stated that the formal records of the Church itself bear out all the gravest charges in contemporary literature.
In 1923, Joseph Kennard wrote that Chaucer’s image of friars as deceitful represents the typical friar as found in real life.
Bishop Moorman, in his _Church Life in England in the Thirteenth Century_ (1945), wrote that the hearing of confessions became increasingly profitable over time, and mendicants jealously claimed the right to do so.
Hinnebusch, in _The Early English Friars Preachers_ (1951), stated that secular clergy formed complaints concerning the administration of the Sacrament of Penance by friars.
Hinnebusch reported that secular clergy claimed friars granted absolution too easily, gave penances that were too light, and made confession an avenue instead of a barrier to sin.
Bowden’s _Commentary on the General Prologue_ (1948/1967) took Chaucer’s portrait of Friar Hubert as representative of the majority of friars.
Bowden stated that Hubert epitomized friars as a class, while also acknowledging that many friars strove to uphold their founders' ideals and compensated for a sinful majority.
Bowden claimed that thousands of friars in fourteenth-century England exhibited guile and covetousness, prompting them to meddle everywhere.
In _The Friars_ (1994), C. H. Lawrence wrote that the allegation of friars letting off penitents too lightly was a commonplace of anti-Mendicant polemics.
Lawrence suggested that the allegation reflected, as much as anything else, the inability of less educated parish clergy to adapt to the new theology of penance.
In _Religion and Devotion in Europe_ (1995), Robert Swanson noted that most people disliked penance and its public nature, not confession itself.
Swanson stated that seculars objected to mendicants giving penances that were too light, but rarely complained that people did not confess.
In 1996, Hagen wrote that Chaucer closely modeled his portraits of Friar Huberd and Friar John on the conventions established by the antifraternal literary tradition.
Hagen acknowledged that something being a literary convention does not necessarily imply that it has no historical basis in fact.
Hagen stated that Chaucer’s attack on the friars has been taken to reflect the real decline of the mendicant orders in the fourteenth century.
The accusation that English friars gave lighter penances than secular clergy, particularly to garner donations, is not supported by the evidence.
The claim "Friars gave light penances to attract donations" is ambiguous due to the complexity of the medieval sacrament of penance.
Bishops’ registers and pastoral manuals are more reliable indicators of administrative reality than other sources.
There is no evidence of substantive differences in the administration of penance by friars and parish clergy concerning:
Whether the theology of penance espoused by the friars justified lighter sentences in general than the theology followed by parish clergy.
Whether friars were too gentle in assigning penances to the wealthy and powerful as a means of courting the donor class.
Whether friars were too lax in requiring penitents, as a condition of their absolution, to pay monetary restitution to people they had harmed or cheated.
Whether friars were claiming the right to absolve penitents of all sins, including those serious sins normally reserved to the bishop’s jurisdiction.

