These writers and critics define the political role of literature as making the invisible visible.
They believe this should be done in a language as transparent as possible.
A range of contemporary French literature challenges this assignment of representativeness.
This literature proposes a different view of the relationship between literature and politics.
This literature advocates for making literature politically rather than political literature.
The article examines some texts by Nathalie Quintane and Sandra Lucbert.
The rejection of literature functioning as a multiplier of visibility and accomplishing a social duty does not imply the mourning of a political action proper to literature.
This rejection positions literature's political action on the side of radical formal invention.
A book titled "Contre la littérature politique" exists.
This book was written by six authors who are among the most far-left writers on the French literary scene.
These authors have expressed their distrust of the adjective "political" when applied to literature.
The adjective "political" has become ubiquitous in academic and media criticism regarding literature.
A remarkable reversal has taken place concerning the "political novel."
The "political novel" now proclaims itself as such and is widely praised.
Jacques Rancière placed "the distribution of the sensible" at the center of his book "_Politics of Literature_".
