Title: Out of Africa: Nat Nakasa's Exit Paperwork
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/17354/
Content: ## Abstract

This article explores the literary imagination that emerged around the paperwork of the South African apartheid state, especially the bureaucratic ephemera—such as _dompasses_, passports, passport applications and exit permits—that limited th

**********Extracted claims**********

The article explores the literary imagination that emerged around the paperwork of the South African apartheid state.
Bureaucratic ephemera, such as _dompasses_, passports, passport applications, and exit permits, limited the free movement of black individuals.
The article examines the case of the South African journalist Nat Nakasa (1937-65).
In Nat Nakasa’s writings, apartheid’s bureaucratic papers are viewed as material curiosities.
Nat Nakasa’s view of bureaucratic papers critiqued the ironic gap between their artificial nature and their hold over individual, predominantly black lives.
After Nat Nakasa’s death in exile, the little magazine he founded, the _Classic_, dedicated a memorial issue to him.
The memorial issue of the _Classic_ republished Nat Nakasa’s writings on the state’s bureaucratic ephemera.
The memorial issue of the _Classic_ preserved Nat Nakasa’s engagements with the state’s bureaucratic ephemera.
The ephemeral nature of the little magazine made it a fitting counter-archive of apartheid.
The _Classic_ registered a sense of the regime’s bureaucratic violence in its fragile material form.
The _Classic_ became a material curiosity from the mid-apartheid years.
The _Classic_ persisted against odds and was kept and archived.
Extensive movement has always been a major prompt for modern literature.
Extensive movement has always been one of modern literature's defining themes.
Jesper Guldall (2015) argued that movement control has been considered ‘anti-narrative’.
In mid-20th-century South Africa, the lack of movement provided an impulse for writing.
The restriction of movement in mid-20th-century South Africa was symptomatic of larger political issues.
Writing in mid-20th-century South Africa often occurred in protest.
Nat Nakasa's subversive pieces from the late 1950s and early 1960s pivoted around encounters with state paperwork, including his own.
Nat Nakasa's pieces were published in black and white-liberal venues such as _Drum_ and the _Rand Daily Mail_.
Following Nat Nakasa’s death in exile in 1965, his pieces were anthologized in a memorial issue of the _Classic_.
The _Classic_ was a little magazine founded by Nat Nakasa.
The _Classic_ paradoxically developed into a counter-archive of apartheid.
Little scholarship exists that is exclusively dedicated to Nat Nakasa.
Existing studies on Nat Nakasa take biographical and historical approaches to his writings (Brown, 2013; Marais, 2016).
Existing studies on Nat Nakasa focus on his tenure as editor of the _Classic_, especially in the context of CIA involvement during the Cold War (Davis, 2020).
The European cultural canon has been studied in light of modernity’s bureaucratic impulses.
Examples of European cultural works studied in this context include Franz Kafka’s _The Castle_, Charles Dickens’s _Bleak House_, and Edgar Degas’s ‘Bureau Pictures’.
The loss of agency in the face of administrative procedures was not exclusively a European experience.
Max Weber (1978) defined bureaucracy as a machine-like ‘structure of domination’.
Across Sub-Saharan Africa, colonisation and the rise of the bureaucratic state were concomitant and entangled phenomena.
Colonisation and the rise of the bureaucratic state each constituted structures of domination in their own right.
Colonisation and the rise of the bureaucratic state each produced a proliferation of paperwork.
The encounter with red tape in Sub-Saharan Africa surpassed mere reading.
Priyasha Mukhopadhyay argued that other forms of engagement with documents existed, such as safekeeping or carrying them around.
These other forms of engagement threw the material forms of documents into sharp relief.
Priyasha Mukhopadhyay (2015) stated that any kind of text, including bureaucratic _paperasse_, may ‘enter human lives and relationships in ways that are not necessarily contingent on their being read’.
Late colonial and early post-colonial subjects were governed through the material practices and artefacts of writing.
Across the continent, writing and power were closely intertwined.
In the Kasai, in colonial Congo, literate Congolese were referred to as _bena mukanda_ (the children of paperwork) (Monaville, 2022).
Kenya under British rule implemented the _kipande_ system.
The _kipande_ system required Kenyans to carry an identity document on their person.
Kenyans were not allowed to move freely without the required identity document.
The pass system in apartheid South Africa is described as the culmination of a project that was stretched to its virtual breaking point.
The paperwork of the apartheid state was ephemeral.
The paperwork was thrown out after it had served its purpose.
Pass application forms were filed away and then forgotten.
Once expired, passbooks and passports were meant to be destroyed.
These documents constituted a form of violence that often remains historically illegible.
Ben Kafka (2009) reminded scholars that they have made ample use of bureaucratic ephemera as sources for the writing of history rather than as sources with their own histories.
Scholars have looked _through_ paperwork, not _at_ it.
To write about these ephemera meant to put this violence on record.
In Nat Nakasa’s journalism, the passbooks, passports, and exit permits of apartheid South Africa emerge as _curiosities_.
Curiosities are defined as objects, often small or intricate, that have a certain interest because of their novelty or strangeness.
For Nat Nakasa, the curiosity lay in the material contours and artificial registers of these documents.
To think of these documents as odd was to critique the ironic gap between their curious, artificial nature and the hold they had over individual, predominantly black lives.
After Nat Nakasa’s refusal of a passport, his subsequent departure on an exit permit, and his death in exile, the _Classic_, his Johannesburg little magazine, dedicated a memorial issue to him.
The memorial issue archived Nat Nakasa’s writings on and engagements with the state’s bureaucratic ephemera.
The _Classic_ was censored, poorly printed, and appeared after long delays.
The _Classic_ became an ephemeral material artefact curiously shaped by its political surroundings.
Magazines were insubstantial, throwaway, dated forms.
Magazines, like passports, expired.
The _Classic_ persisted despite the death of its founding editor.
By persisting, the _Classic_ reclaimed something of its ephemeral nature as a statement against power.
The affective purchase of the little magazine made it a fitting counter-archive of apartheid.
The _Classic_ was an alternative site that challenged the state’s narrative record.
The _Classic_ preserved and countered a sense of the regime’s bureaucratic violence in its fragile material form.
The article gestures beyond Nat Nakasa to a wider literary landscape.
Key writers of the period included Nadine Gordimer, Lewis Nkosi, Doris Lessing, Chinua Achebe, William Plomer, and Richard Rive.
A creative, subversive engagement with the ephemera of the state sits as one of the defining motifs in the postcolonial canon.

