Title: Appreciation of Black Humour Memes in the Context of the Russo-Ukrainian War
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16523/
Content: ## Abstract

This study examines three black humour memes and their reception on social media during the 2022 Russian full-scale invasion of Ukraine. It seeks to identify the conditions that facilitate or impede the appreciation of jokes about the enemy’s

**********Extracted claims**********

The study examines three black humour memes and their reception on social media during the 2022 Russian full-scale invasion of Ukraine.
The study aims to identify the conditions that facilitate or impede the appreciation of jokes about the enemy’s death.
Conceptual integration theory and Bandura’s social cognitive theory of moral agency provide the methodological framework for analysing the memes and responses to them.
The findings offer an explanatory model for understanding when aggressive jokes are well-received or rejected.
Successful humour transactions often involve humourists providing cues that encourage viewers to deactivate empathy towards the joke’s target.
Cues for deactivating empathy may include euphemistic labelling, dehumanisation, or shared conventions about the consequences of the humour.
Such triggers align with Bandura’s patterns of moral disengagement.
The interaction between these triggers and the background knowledge of the audience can lead to the appreciation of the humour or a conflict of values that results in the joke being dismissed.
Humour is generally well-received when both the humourists and the audience agree on the need to undermine the power of the target.
Humour is generally well-received when both the humourists and the audience believe in the delivery of retribution for perceived wrongs.
If the target is perceived as valiant or vulnerable, the humour is often rejected as offensive or in poor taste.
Scholars have often noted the use of aggressive humour as a coping mechanism for individuals facing difficult circumstances.
Humour may convey hope for a better future.
Humour may suppress fear.
Humour may bond like-minded individuals.
Humour may provide a vent for aggression.
During the Holocaust, Jews reportedly joked about Hitler’s death as a form of resistance.
Soviet dissidents and Gulag prisoners used jokes about Stalin to maintain morale.
Obrdlík described how ‘gallows humour’ helped Czechs to forge in-group cohesion during the German occupation.
Gallows humour in the context of the German occupation included jokes about gravediggers and cemeteries offering exclusive services for Hitler and Germans.
Obrdlík argued that the term gallows humour had typically been used in relation to the humour of nations experiencing oppression from invaders.
The literal meaning of gallows humour evokes the scene of innocent victims sent to the gallows by a dictator but still hoping for a rescue miracle.
Gallows humour was adopted as a tool of psychological liberation in oppressive circumstances.
By mocking their persecutors, the oppressed asserted their resilience and contempt towards the aggressors.
If gallows humour conventionally refers to jokes made by the oppressed, black humour describes tragic and sinister subjects such as death, disease, handicap, or warfare.
It is difficult to locate authors of jokes about disastrous matters, as such jokes can travel across time and cultures.
Black humour becomes a more useful term when dealing with gruesome topics when the authors of the joke cannot be found.
Both black humour and gallows humour allow those in dire situations to build a sense of superiority over their adversaries.
The function of black humour became indispensable when the Ukrainian sphere of social media was shaping its reaction to the Russian invasion on 24 February 2022.
The Russian invasion on 24 February 2022 was a significant escalation of the Russo-Ukrainian War that began in 2014.
Ukrainians widely employed black humour through memes to show their defiance and to disparage the invaders.
Memes effectively combined visual and verbal elements to convey messages that might be deemed too harsh or unacceptable if expressed solely in words.
Memes are virally-transmitted cultural artefacts reflecting shared social norms and values.
Memes have become a powerful medium for both storytelling and social commentary.
Many memes disseminated during the Russo-Ukrainian war were novel.
Numerous memes adapted recyclable images that had been previously used as building blocks in digital jokes.
Anonymous meme creators modified existing verbal jokes about past events and political leaders.
Such adaptations demonstrate the continuity of the practice of using black humour in response to oppression and traumatic events.
A notable example of meme adaptation is one disseminated in pro-Ukrainian sections of Facebook that repurposed a Jewish joke about the day of Hitler’s death.
The Jewish joke told a story about Hitler consulting an astrologer about the date of his death and receiving the enigmatic answer that his death would occur on a big Jewish holiday that would be proclaimed the very day he died.
Ukrainian or pro-Ukrainian netizens translated the Jewish joke into Ukrainian and replaced culture-specific names with Russo-Ukrainian equivalents.
The creators of the meme illustrated the verbal joke with a picture of Alexei Navalny putting flowers next to Putin’s coffin in Red Square.
New variants of jokes do not change the plot of old jokes but align existing perspectives with a modern outlook.
Sometimes, new variants amend the preceding storyline of old jokes.
The practice of using and recycling memes for social commentary exemplifies stages of humour transaction.
Humour transaction is defined as processes by which what is generally called humour is created, communicated, experienced, responded to, and used.
Adoption of the humour transaction concept into a research framework enables the assessment of different issues related to the production and reception of jokes, including those with aggressive references.
The topic of physical violence has been actively explored in memes whose protagonists were either fictional characters representing soldiers, zealous patriots and their children, or recognisable individuals.
The targeting of specific individuals is usually associated with immoral practices such as bullying and harassment.
At a time of war, any harm to the enemy camp is conventionally perceived as a desirable development mostly beyond reprehension.
Questions arise about whether alleged perpetrators should be protected by rules of ‘civilised’ communication imposed by some social media platforms, even in the context of war.
Another contentious issue concerning the use of hostile jokes arises with respect to the targets at whose expense jokes can be made.
Some participants in public discussions of memes accuse creators of black humour memes and their supporters of different types of asocial behaviour.
Kuipers concluded that the function of aggressive memes could be viewed differently by different people and often fluctuates between coping and expressing hostility.
A study of hostile humorous memes and reactions helps to uncover the boundaries of jokes beyond which a humourist risks inflicting adversarial reactions and provoking social sanctions.
Memes in which the death of the target was sought after were selected for the analysis in this study.
The selected memes celebrate retribution instantiated in a metaphorical death sentence for violent aggressors or those perceived as such.
The messages of these memes suggest that invaders, their leaders, and even passive bystanders will be held accountable for war crimes committed by their compatriots.
Themes used in these memes often draw on expressions like the Russian idiom ‘Whoever comes to us with a sword will perish by the sword’ and similar sentiments from the New Testament.
Creators and appreciative audiences of these memes denigrate alleged evil perpetrators who lack humanity and are undeserving of empathy.
This investigation uses three memes containing aggressive and disparaging references as case studies to explore the acceptance and rejection of black humour in the context of war.
One meme depicts a creative method for assassinating the Russian President by using a shopping bag.
Another meme references a Ukrainian fundraiser’s initiative to hire a fictional hitman to end the war.
The third meme portrays a mission assigned by the Ukrainian Security Service to an anthropomorphised shark.
These case studies aim to shed light on conditions that lead to successful humour transactions endorsing aggressive jocular statements.
These case studies aim to shed light on conditions that lead to transactions resulting in the rejection of the vengeance narrative.
The study investigates whether fragmentations of the discourse community engaged in discussions of memes could correlate with patterns of moral disengagement explored by Albert Bandura and his theories.
Bandura’s socio-cognitive explanatory model shows that the justification of violence and death typically involves a process of self-regulation.
Through self-regulation, individuals attempt to validate their aggression to make their ruthless behaviour socially acceptable.
Disengagement involves several socio-cognitive mechanisms observed in studies of bullying, harassment, excessive punitive responses to tragic events, and the execution of criminal orders.
Millner Davis extends the application of Bandura’s theory to instances of production and reception of hostile humour, laying the groundwork for exploring positive aspects of moral disengagement.
Positive aspects of moral disengagement may involve efforts to cope with extreme stress and to create a bond with a community of shared values.
The study analyses the structure of aggressive memes and catalogues the verbal and visual responses to them.
The aim is to check which patterns of moral disengagement gained relevance and why these mechanisms were not activated in the comments of those who rejected the humour.
