Title: ‘If parody does not prickle it does not work’: Reflections on the Interpretive Challenges of Dark Parody in the Dutch and South African Courts
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16695/
Content: ## Abstract

Focusing on the nexus of humour and the right to free speech, this article introduces the concept of ‘dark parody’ to address parodies that reuse existing material with a dark, humorous twist and explores how courts deal with two of the main 

**********Extracted claims**********

The article explores the relationship between humor and the right to free speech.
The article introduces the concept of ‘dark parody’.
Dark parody refers to parodies that reuse existing material with a dark, humorous twist.
The article examines how courts handle two main interpretive challenges presented by ‘dark parody’.
The first interpretive challenge is interpreting the distance between the parody and the copyright/trademark protected work it parodies.
The second interpretive challenge is interpreting the dark humor used in a parody.
The article uses a meta-hermeneutic approach.
The article analyzes how the main features of parody, legal criteria applied to parody in courts, and the role of dark humor influence judicial interpretation regarding (dark) parody in copyright and trademark law.
The article proposes the theoretical framework of ‘parodic distance’.
The framework of ‘parodic distance’ aims to address the interpretive challenges systematically presented by dark parody.
The framework includes six main categories: content, style, context, message, function, and affect.
These six categories can be used to interpret the distance between a parody and the original work.
The article applies this framework to two case studies: Mercis c.s. v. Punt.nl (Court of Appeal of Amsterdam) and Laugh It Off Promotions v. South African Breweries (Constitutional Court of South Africa).
The article provides a comparative analysis of how these courts currently deal with interpretive issues surrounding dark humor in copyright and trademark law.
The article shows that the framework of parodic distance can be a useful conceptual tool.
The framework provides a shared vocabulary to complement judicial interpretation and legal discourse at the intersection of dark parody and the law.
Parody is a humorous expression of artistic creativity.
Parody invites critical and reflective thinking about topics and tensions in democratic societies.
Judge Sachs suggested that parody needs to 'prickle' to be effective (para. 75, Laugh It Off Promotions v. South African Breweries).
Parody provokes, confronts, and challenges its audience.
Parody is defined as a creative work that imitates an original work in a recognizable manner.
Parody simultaneously transforms aspects of the original work in various ways.
Parody often has humorous intent or effect.
Parody can fulfill a range of functions.
Parody has the ability to evoke many different emotions from an audience.
Legal scholars agree that parody plays an important role in a democratic society.
Parody can express critical commentary on social issues.
Parody exists within the tension between the right to free speech (Art. 19 Universal Declaration of Human Rights) and the right to intellectual property (Art. 17 UDHR).
This tension is particularly relevant for instances of dark parody.
Dark parody imitates an existing work and transforms it with dark humor.
Dark parody can use various types of dark humor.
Dark parody can relocate an original work into the context of a socially or culturally taboo situation or event.
Dark humor is tentatively defined by Godioli (2024) as a type of humor where at least one of the scripts is normally associated with feelings of sadness, grief, or horror (129).
The article mainly focuses on interpretive challenges of dark parody in free speech and intellectual property (IP) law.
Dark parody also presents challenges concerning free speech and other fundamental rights, such as the right to dignity (Art. 1 UDHR).
Dark parody can function as a vehicle for communicating implicitly discriminatory messages, blurring boundaries with hate speech.
The article argues that interpreting the distance between a (dark) parody and the original work and interpreting dark humor are two main interpretive challenges for courts dealing with dark parody in copyright and trademark infringement allegations.
Dark parody is an ideal case study to explore these two interpretive challenges within the broader framework of humorous expression as part of the fundamental human right to freedom of expression.
In parody disputes, judges must balance the interests of copyright and trademark rights holders against the parodist’s right to freedom of expression.
These interpretive challenges play an important role in judicial interpretation during this balancing exercise.
The theoretical approach used to reflect on the judicial interpretive process concerning dark parody is meta-hermeneutics.
Meta-hermeneutics is a term coined by Korthals Altes (2014) in narratology.
Korthals Altes defines meta-hermeneutics as an approach and type of reflection that relates interpretive argumentations to their underlying value-laden conceptions and pathways (99).
Adapting meta-hermeneutics to the nexus of humor and the law, its application to judicial interpretation practices focuses on reconstructing conventional paths along which judges and courts construct meaning.
It investigates concepts used by courts.
It explores why, when, and how courts interpret a parody as ‘distant enough’ from the original work to be allowed as a humorous expression of IP-protected material.
The interpretation of the distance between a (dark) parody and an original work plays a fundamental role in judicial decision-making.
This interpretation provides the grounds for the decision to allow or prohibit a parody.
A systematic approach to interpreting this distance is currently lacking in judicial interpretive practices.
The lack of a systematic approach can lead to inconsistent outcomes.
The article aims to create a theoretical framework to address the lack of a systematic test for interpreting the distance between a (dark) parody and an original work.
The framework integrates insights from humanities-based humor research and legal scholarship on intellectual property law.
The article has a strong theoretical focus.
The case studies primarily illustrate the workings of the theoretical framework.
The resulting framework is intended as a conceptual tool to support courts in developing an interpretation that considers all complexities of the (dark) parody under review.
The framework aims to create a stable ground for judicial decision-making impacting freedom of expression.
This is especially important when dealing with dark parody, as dark humor adds another layer of complexity to interpreting its meaning and impact.
Through their dark humor, dark parodies touch upon ‘metapragmatic stereotypes’ of humor.
Tsakona (2020) defines ‘metapragmatic stereotypes’ as internalized models of humor people have, and ‘how it should be used, when, why, and for what purposes’ (16).
Dark parodies deal with the legal framework of intellectual property law and the right to freedom of expression.
Dark parodies also relate to issues of morality, cultural values, and societal beliefs about humor.
The first section of the article shows how courts' interpretation of parody is based on the resonances between the main features of parody and legal criteria in copyright and trademark law.
The first section introduces the concept of ‘dark parody’.
The concept of dark parody illustrates how parody can be a medium for expressing different types of dark humor.
Based on these resonances, the second section proposes the interpretive framework of ‘parodic distance’.
The framework of ‘parodic distance’ offers a systematic approach to interpreting both the distance between a dark parody and the original and the type of dark humor used.
The last section provides a comparative analysis of two case studies revolving around dark parody.
The case studies are Mercis c.s. v. Punt.nl (Court of Appeal of Amsterdam, LJN:BS7825, 13 September 2011) and Laugh It Off Promotions v. South African Breweries (Constitutional Court of South Africa, CCT42/04, 27 May 2005).
A close reading and comparative analysis of the courts' interpretive behavior in both cases offers a deeper understanding of the values underlying the legal protection of dark parody in both legal systems.

