Title: BS Johnson and the Attack of the White-Collar Working Class
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/11618/
Content: ## Abstract

The conventional wisdom around working-class writing often presupposes a realist aesthetic form, a hero employed in manual labour and, in the British context, a setting somewhere in its former industrial heartlands, certainly no further south

**********Extracted claims**********

Conventional wisdom regarding working-class writing often presupposes a realist aesthetic form.
Conventional wisdom regarding working-class writing often presupposes a hero employed in manual labor.
In the British context, conventional wisdom regarding working-class writing often presupposes a setting in former industrial heartlands, generally no further south than the Midlands (excluding South Wales Valleys).
The writing of B.S. Johnson conforms to none of these conventional assumptions about working-class writing.
B.S. Johnson's writing has consistently been overlooked within the field of working-class literary studies.
B.S. Johnson has a biographical claim to inclusion within the field of working-class literary studies.
B.S. Johnson's novels are firmly rooted in a form of class politics.
The class nature of B.S. Johnson’s novels is not diminished by his depiction of white-collar characters.
B.S. Johnson's depiction of white-collar characters allows for an understanding of the essential nature of alienated labor and class antagonism in capitalist society, even within Britain's postwar welfare state.
B.S. Johnson’s avant-garde aesthetics and recurring motif of white-collar employment mobilize political themes in his novels.
B.S. Johnson's novels should be understood as distinctly working-class novels.
The 21st century has seen a revival of interest in B.S. Johnson.
Almost all of B.S. Johnson's novels have returned to print.
Jonathan Coe wrote the biography _Like a Fiery Elephant: The Story of B.S. Johnson_ (2004).
B.S. Johnson is a figure in Britain’s postwar avant-garde.
B.S. Johnson is noted for both his working-class background and his vociferous espousal of aesthetic innovation.
B.S. Johnson's father was a bookseller’s stockkeeper.
B.S. Johnson's mother was variously a waitress, barmaid, and between-maid.
B.S. Johnson is often understood as both working class and a writer.
B.S. Johnson is rarely considered a ‘working-class writer’ in the same vein as Robert Tressell or Alan Sillitoe.
This rare consideration is significantly due to issues of aesthetics and motifs in B.S. Johnson's work.
Working-class writing has conventionally been considered a realist endeavor.
B.S. Johnson’s avant-gardism means his work falls outside of what is conventionally considered ‘working-class writing’.
B.S. Johnson’s protagonists are often white-collar workers.
Manual laborers are commonly associated with working-class fiction.
B.S. Johnson’s works are read as reflecting his philosophical or aesthetic interests, rather than explicit class concerns.
B.S. Johnson's 1973 novel, _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_, is a slight exception to the perception that his works lack explicit class concerns.
Even _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ is largely coded as a ‘political’ rather than ‘class’ novel.
Class concerns, particularly around the theme of alienated labor, are present in a number of B.S. Johnson’s novels.
_Albert Angelo_ (1964) and _The Unfortunates_ (1969) are examples of Johnson’s novels that contain class concerns and alienated labor.
_Albert Angelo_ mobilizes avant-gardism as part of its exposition of its protagonist’s alienation from employment as a supply teacher.
B.S. Johnson uses formal devices in _The Unfortunates_ to highlight the inability of relatively well-remunerated white-collar work (journalism) to resolve the essentially alienating nature of waged labor.
_The Unfortunates_, while rooted in the experience of waged work, moves towards a broader conception of social antagonism that includes issues of gender and consumption.
White-collar waged work and its refusal are major themes in B.S. Johnson's _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_.
White-collar waged work and its refusal form the bedrock of _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_'s social critique.
_Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ points towards a positive class politics.
B.S. Johnson’s other texts often deal with class-related themes.
Aspects of autobiography are present in B.S. Johnson's _Trawl_ (1966).
Elderly care is a theme in B.S. Johnson's _House Mother Normal_ (1971).
B.S. Johnson's mother’s work in kitchens is a theme in _See the Old Lady Decently_ (1975).
The novels _Albert Angelo_, _The Unfortunates_, and _Christie Malry’s Own Double-Entry_ were chosen for discussion in this article due to the centrality of white-collar waged work in them.
The type of waged work (white-collar) in these selected novels, alongside their avant-gardism, underpins their radical nature.
Gilles Dauvé states that identifying "proletarian" with "factory worker" misses what is subversive in the proletarian condition.
Reading B.S. Johnson’s novels as radical reappraisals of the proletarian condition and its subversiveness allows for a broadening of common sense notions of what ‘working-class literature’ looks like.
Reading B.S. Johnson’s novels in this way allows B.S. Johnson to be situated firmly within working-class literature.

