Title: Maps and the Burdens of Belonging: Identity and Resistance in Gurnah’s By the Sea and Owuor’s The Dragonfly Sea
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16725/
Content: ## Abstract

This article examines the relationship between maps, identity, and belonging in two post-colonial  novels set in the Western Indian Ocean region: Abdulrazak Gurnah’s _By the Sea_ (2001) and Yvonne Adhiambo Owuor’s _The Dragonfly Sea_ (2019). 

**********Extracted claims**********

The article examines the relationship between maps, identity, and belonging in two post-colonial novels set in the Western Indian Ocean region: Abdulrazak Gurnah’s _By the Sea_ (2001) and Yvonne Adhiambo Owuor’s _The Dragonfly Sea_ (2019).
Maps are portrayed in these novels not merely as static remnants of colonialism but as dynamic artifacts.
Maps both reflect and shape post-colonial identities in nuanced ways within the novels.
The novels depict maps as having a degree of agency.
Maps are capable of both oppressing and offering pathways to self-discovery for post-colonial subjects, as depicted in the novels.
The article highlights themes of naming, representation, and identity concerning maps across both texts.
A coherent cross-reading of the plurality and contestation inherent in the post-colonial reading of maps is possible.
In Abdulrazak Gurnah’s novel _By the Sea_ (2001), the protagonist Latif Mahmud grieves the loss of his family’s material possessions.
Latif Mahmud emotionally frames Saleh Omar as an ‘assassin’ who takes over the family home through duplicitous business dealings in _By the Sea_.
Latif Mahmud believes Saleh Omar removed his family from their possessions and ended their lives as they knew it.
The reaction of Latif Mahmud reveals an underlying logic that closely intertwines the family’s possessions with their identity.
The confiscation of objects is positioned as deeply damaging to the family's sense of self in _By the Sea_.
Latif Mahmud paradoxically deems the possessions ‘debris’.
The material objects in the family home of Zanzibar inhabitants constitute oceanic flotsams from around the world.
Saleh Omar deals in merchandise as an antiquarian.
The emotionally charged rhetoric of ‘assassin’ followed by ‘debris’ elucidates paradoxical associations between materiality and identity in _By the Sea_.
The loss of materiality can profoundly undermine identity, as shown in _By the Sea_.
Physical objects carry intricate feelings and attachments.
Maps function as multifaceted artifacts that reflect and profoundly shape post-colonial identities, as argued in the article.
Maps played multifaceted roles in colonial and post-colonial contexts.
Originally tools for navigation, maps evolved into instruments of racial and regional classification during the colonial era.
Maps continued to exert influence in post-colonial spaces.
Colonial maps persist in racializing the Indian Ocean region despite outliving their primary navigational function.
The novels examined do not reference specific historical maps.
The novels use various cartographic objects to illuminate characters’ emotional and psychological experiences within the Indian Ocean world.
The novels explore the complex interplay between geography, race, and colonial legacies in shaping individual and collective identities.
The novels position maps as potent yet ephemeral artifacts that play a crucial role in characters’ understanding of themselves and their place in the world.
_By the Sea_ follows the story of an academic, Latif Mahmud, who is settled in England.
Latif Mahmud is introduced to a refugee named Saleh Omar, an antiquarian from Zanzibar, who has recently arrived in the UK.
Latif Mahmud is surprised to learn that Saleh Omar arrived in the UK using his father’s name.
Latif Mahmud and Saleh Omar reveal unpleasant shared histories of their families before and after the Zanzibari revolution and their experiences post-exile.
The dialogues between Latif Mahmud and Saleh Omar excavate suppressed memories of loss and displacement conditioned by Zanzibar’s fractured decolonization.
Saleh Omar uses maps extensively to recount his experiences in _By the Sea_.
_The Dragonfly Sea_ traces the life of Ayaana, born from a socially ostracized affair between Munira and an unnamed father in Kenya’s littoral zone.
Under the wing of Muhidin, a widely traveled sailor, Ayaana’s maturation interweaves her emergent identity with rich Swahili poetic traditions.
Muhidin immerses Ayaana in Islamic verses, Arabic and Persian poetry, and Bollywood songs.
Bollywood songs eerily prefigure Ayaana’s ancestral ties with Ming-dynasty Chinese seafarers along the coast in _The Dragonfly Sea_.
Ayaana’s odyssey to China for advanced studies crystallizes the Indian Ocean’s fluidity as a conduit of cross-cultural fusion and fragmentation.
The fluidity of the Indian Ocean is inscribed through centuries into East Africa’s littoral communities.
The article uses Bill Brown’s views on objects varying in significance based on their context and lens to understand how maps in these novels symbolize shifting identities.
Bill Brown posits that objects are different things in different scenes, however materially stable they may seem.
Latif Mahmud’s interactions with maps exemplify Bill Brown's idea about objects.
Graham Huggan argues that maps are pivotal as they both reflect and perpetuate colonial power dynamics by normalizing Western superiority and marginalizing non-Western perspectives.
A post-colonial analysis of maps reveals the underlying power structures embedded within ostensibly objective records.
Examining maps challenges the self-legitimizing authority of Western cartography and contests its claims to universality.
The process of decolonizing maps necessitates identifying and repudiating inscribed colonial ideologies.
Decolonizing maps involves interrogating how cartography has been used to rationalize colonial expansion, erase indigenous knowledge and territories, and impose Western paradigms on non-Western societies.
In the novels examined, a coherent sense of self eludes the characters.
A coherent sense of self cannot be attained through interpersonal interactions within colonial or post-colonial contexts, for the characters in the novels.
The characters’ sole perceived pathway to accessing conceptual frameworks and situating themselves within broader epistemologies and discourses appears to lie in communication with the objects themselves.
The interaction between objects and subjects engenders the construction of important issues.
This interaction prompts questions that post-colonial subjects lack resources to address.
The discursive interplay between objects and subjects constitutes the focal point of the article's analysis.
The implications of this dialogue posit the objects as possessing a degree of willful agency.
Objects are capable of not merely transmitting but also receiving meaningful messages.
The article introduces a framework for exploring colonial mappings in Gurnah’s novel.
The article provides an extended analysis of the anthropomorphized agency afforded to maps within Gurnah's narrative.
The article situates Owuor’s novel within post-colonial examinations of the politics of erasure underlying cartographic practices.
The second section explores the protagonist’s quest to resituate her island home within the cartographic record.
The protagonist’s quest serves as a metaphor for the broader struggle to rediscover African identities obscured by imperial/colonial mappings.

