Title: Humour from the Right: Authoritarian Populism and Punch-Down Laughter in India
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16778/
Content: ## Abstract

This paper examines India’s authoritarian populist politics through a study of humorous speeches made by the nation’s prime minister, Narendra Modi, known for his Hindu supremacist right-wing stance. It qualitatively analyses Modi’s strategic

**********Extracted claims**********

The paper examines India’s authoritarian populist politics.
The examination is conducted through a study of humorous speeches made by Prime Minister Narendra Modi.
Narendra Modi is known for his Hindu supremacist right-wing stance.
The paper qualitatively analyzes Modi’s strategic use of humor.
Modi’s humor targets marginalized groups, including intellectuals, women, people with disabilities, and the Muslim community.
The act of suicide and the state of widowhood have also been targets of Modi’s humor.
The study employs textual analysis of specific jokes and insults delivered by Modi in public forums.
The study demonstrates how carnivalesque speech is used to ‘punch down’ on individuals and groups based on their identity.
The paper draws a comparison with Donald Trump’s use of similar humor.
Both Modi and Trump utilize humor to destabilize their opposition and mock minorities.
The paper uses Mikhail Bakhtin’s carnival theory as a lens.
Bakhtin’s carnival theory originally posits a democratic and inclusive potential to subvert power.
The study argues that the ‘inverted carnival’ employed by leaders like Modi and Trump serves to consolidate power and undermine minority rights.
This research introduces the concept of ‘inverted carnival’ as a new framework.
The ‘inverted carnival’ framework is for analyzing the use of humor in authoritarian populist governance globally.
India’s Prime Minister, Narendra Modi, has tweeted about the need for more satire and humor in public life.
Modi emphasizes humor’s power to bring happiness and healing.
In January 2017, Modi tweeted from his @PMOIndia handle: ‘I think we need more satire and humour. Humour brings happiness in our lives. Humour is the best healer: PM @narendramodi’.
In March 2017, Modi tweeted from his @narendramodi handle: ‘We surely need more humour in public life’.
Modi has expressed a clear preference for humor.
Modi contends that humor has uplifting and restorative powers and stresses its importance in public life.
Modi has made jokes at public events and electoral rallies.
Modi has called his political rivals names.
Modi has targeted scholarly figures, women, disabled people, and Muslims in his humor.
Modi is not known to make self-deprecatory jokes often or effectively whilst addressing audiences in India.
Modi has used self-mockery during his visit to the US.
Modi has used delightful rhetoric to entertain his audience in Australia.
Modi’s political style has been described as authoritarian populist.
Authoritarian populism is increasingly understood as a widespread international trend towards illiberalism.
Illiberal politics is rooted in setting one group of people against another by a leadership.
The leadership in illiberal politics represents itself as the voice of the ‘common people’.
The leadership in illiberal politics portrays a struggle against ‘the elite’ and minority groups.
The leader in authoritarian populism is represented as a ‘strongman’.
The leader in authoritarian populism possesses a cult-like personality.
The leader in authoritarian populism embodies the will of the common people.
The trend of authoritarian populism has degraded democratic discourse.
The trend of authoritarian populism has fueled nationalism.
The trend of authoritarian populism has obscured the lines between truth and fiction.
The 45th president of the USA, Donald Trump, is also described as an authoritarian populist.
Donald Trump used mocking humor as a political weapon.
Donald Trump used mocking humor to destabilize his opponents and wear down democracy while reveling in it.
Donald Trump is known for his disregard of minority rights.
Donald Trump is known for his ridicule of women and immigrants.
Donald Trump has been accused of mocking a disabled reporter.
Trump and Modi seem to play a curious carnival game of strongman mockery.
The strongman mockery by Trump and Modi is aimed at belittling minority groups and the so-called establishment.
The strongman mockery by Trump and Modi aims to engender and maintain popular electoral support.
The historical origins of Modi’s party, the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), in the 1920s, have been linked to ideological sympathies with European fascism.
The BJP and its leadership are widely recognized for their right-wing politics in the present times.
Russian philosopher and literary critic Mikhail Bakhtin (1895–1975) conceptualized carnival as a popular festive form.
Carnival, as conceptualized by Bakhtin, symbolizes the inverting of accepted social order.
Within Bakhtin’s carnival framework, laughter is a form of resistance to power.
Laughter in Bakhtin’s framework engenders a sense of subversion from below.
Bakhtin’s carnival model has been commended for its democratic ethic.
Bakhtin’s carnival model has been commended for its potential to open up spaces that challenge hierarchies.
Scholars have studied Bakhtin’s work in the context of India.
Connections have been drawn between Bakhtin’s concepts and the ancient philosophical traditions of India.
This study examines aspects of ‘punch-down’ humor in Modi’s speeches through Bakhtin’s carnival model.
The study seeks to understand the objective behind Modi’s humor.
Modi’s humor targets disability, mental health, suicide, intellectualism, women, widowhood, and the Indian National Congress Party.
The paper analyzes how humor is wielded by India’s populist leadership from their own position of power.
The populist leaders seemingly punch _up_ at the ‘establishment’ represented by the Nehru-Gandhi dynastic members of the Congress.
In Bakhtin’s model, the common ‘folk’ were allowed carnival as an event where they could mock authority figures.
In the contexts of Modi and Trump, both powerful individuals, humor is used to punch _down_.
As populist politicians, Modi and Trump have _avowedly_ positioned themselves as outsiders located on the margins.
From their perceived marginal position, Modi and Trump _appear_ to be punching _up_ at the establishment.
This punching up seemingly shapes public opinion and influences electoral outcomes.
This paper argues that this kind of carnivalesque use is essentially a co-option of Bakhtin’s carnival vision for undemocratic gains.
This use is an inversion of the Bakhtinian carnival inversion.
This inversion brings one back to the status quo.
Instead of punching up to subvert from below, this form of carnivalesque punches down to reinforce hierarchies.
This co-opted model of carnival inversion is a novel framework.
The novel framework may be useful in studies of punch-down humor used by politicians around the world.

