Title: Photo Archives and Linked Open Data. The Added Value
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/15232/
Content: ## Abstract

In the last two decades, cultural heritage institutions have been revisiting the way they publish their data. Due to the rise of Semantic Web technologies and graph-based search engines, the shift in the technology stack has required many to 

**********Extracted claims**********

Cultural heritage institutions have revisited their methods for publishing data.
The rise of Semantic Web technologies and graph-based search engines has led many institutions to reconsider their data organization.
Data literacy skills have developed among cataloguers, archivists, and collection managers as a byproduct of this shift.
Institutions were promised an improved image in terms of authoritativeness due to enhanced data quality.
Institutions were promised increased attractiveness to patrons due to enhanced search capabilities.
Photo archives have embraced a new paradigm for data publication.
The ZERI & LODe project is dedicated to publishing the catalogue of the Federico Zeri Photo Archive as Linked Open Data.
Organizations in various knowledge domains have had to revise their technology stack and knowledge organization arrangements due to the rise of Semantic Web technologies.
The Cultural Heritage domain has been an enthusiastic adopter of the Linked Open Data (LOD) movement.
The Cultural Heritage domain represents a major application field for Linked Open Data (LOD).
Libraries, archives, and museums (LAMs) have broadly adopted the new paradigm, albeit with diverse degrees of implementation.
Scholarship in Cultural Analytics and Digital Humanities contains many case studies demonstrating institutions' successful adoption of the Semantic Web.
Projects typically address technical challenges arising from legacy data and software solutions.
These projects often present new data sources, tools, or frameworks to solve research problems.
A common goal for these projects is to achieve benefits promised by the Semantic Web.
The promised benefits of the Semantic Web include improved information retrieval, facilitated record linking, improved visibility, and better analytics and services/applications.
Extensive discussion and evaluation of the achievements of Semantic Web projects are not found in the literature.
Projects often demonstrate reaching technological goals but do not provide evidence of achieving promised benefits or the extent of those benefits.
Traditional research methods are still employed in online catalogues despite the promise of better analytics and new services.
Small institutions that rely on aggregators for data publication cannot afford to develop tools tailored to their collections.
Photo archives serve as a representative, yet understudied, example in this context.
Photo archives increasingly make their collections available through digital catalogues.
Digital catalogues support scholars in iconographic and historiographic research.
Art historical photo archives are attracted to Linked Open Data (LOD).
LOD is seen as a method to address the lack of structured metadata regarding image content in art historical photo archives.
LOD is considered a common language (lingua franca) to integrate photo collections across institutions.
The integration of photo collections via LOD aims to support scholars with advanced search capabilities.
The ZERI & LODe project is an example of Linked Open Data for a small, renowned art historical photo archive.
The ZERI & LODe project has been running for a sufficient period to allow for discussion of its achievements and limitations.
Some considerations derived from the examined domain/example are of general interest and warrant further investigation in other fields.

