Title: Katherine Anne Porter’s Ship of Fools and the Ephemeral Promise of Transnational Community
URL: https://olh.openlibhums.org/article/id/16531/
Content: ## Abstract

In the 1920s and 1930s, transatlantic steam travel was at its fastest and most accessible. During voyages lasting mere days or weeks, shipping lines used onboard aesthetics and ephemera to construct community, attempting to replace national b

**********Extracted claims**********

In the 1920s and 1930s, transatlantic steam travel reached its fastest and most accessible point.
During voyages, shipping lines utilized onboard aesthetics and ephemera to foster community among passengers.
Shipping lines attempted to replace passengers' national belonging with transnational solidarity.
Katherine Anne Porter’s novel _Ship of Fools_, published in 1962, suggests that attempts to transcend national loyalties could not counter rising international tensions of the period.
Katherine Anne Porter largely wrote _Ship of Fools_ after a 1931 trip.
Sinclair Lewis’s 1929 novel _Dodsworth_ includes ocean liner passengers humorously observing "their fellow-citizens in this brave village amid the desert of waters."
F. Scott Fitzgerald’s 1934 novel _Tender is the Night_ refers to a liner's inhabitants as "citizen\[s\] of a commonwealth smaller than Andorra."
The novels _Dodsworth_ and _Tender is the Night_ emphasize the attachments formed aboard ships when transatlantic passenger travel was at its peak in popularity and ease.
At sea, travelers were placed into community with people from various parts of the world.
In the shipboard environment, travelers were encouraged to temporarily substitute passengers' national belonging with shipboard solidarity.
The finite nature of transatlantic crossings meant that bonds among passengers had to be established quickly.
These shipboard bonds might dissipate rapidly upon disembarkation.
Lisa Gitelman states in her book that "documents are integral to…the social order that \[people\] inhabit."
Shipboard materials clearly define the parameters of a short-lived society.
Placing the name of the shipping line or liner prominently throughout the ship was a method to invite passengers into community with their fellow travelers.
The line or liner name was engraved on glassware, painted on dishes, woven into curtains, carved into furniture, embroidered on ribbons, and stamped on playing cards, napkins, and stationery.
The repetition of the line or liner name provided passengers with visual reminders of their new, temporary affiliation.
The record of shipboard solidarity persists in discussions of transient belonging found in novels of the era, including _Tender is the Night_ and _Dodsworth_.
The record of shipboard solidarity also persists in travel ephemera.
Travel ephemera can be read in parallel with the narrative accounts found in novels.
Katherine Anne Porter’s 1962 novel _Ship of Fools_ engages with both shipboard community narration and the incorporation of shipboard ephemera.
_Ship of Fools_ is the only long novel written by Katherine Anne Porter.
Katherine Anne Porter is a Pulitzer Prize-winning American author best known for her short stories.
_Ship of Fools_ is set aboard a steamship in 1931.
The fictional S.S. _Vera_ in _Ship of Fools_ sails from Mexico to Germany.
_Ship of Fools_ preserves in language documents designed to become the detritus of travel.
_Ship of Fools_ references tickets, seating cards, cabin cards, shipboard newspapers, and lunch and dinner menus.
Porter identifies the steamship as a contained society where passengers adopt new commitments.
Porter describes the steamship as a "temporary haven" on the opening page of _Ship of Fools_.
Porter suggests that documents create a social order.
The beginning of _Ship of Fools_ indicates that the _Vera_’s passengers hoped their papers might establish a momentary immunity from the hazards of their enterprise.
In Porter’s book, documents ultimately betray their bearers.
Documents in Porter's book are as likely to cause division as to build solidarity.
Porter uses the ship as a setting to depict the failure of the international project.
The setting of Porter's novel is a period of transnational promise but also increasing national tensions.

